  "total_cost_usd": 0.30592600000000003,
  "subtype": "success",
  "final_text": "I submitted **holistic**, with confidence 0.85. I used 57 of the 60 budget.\n\n**Design:** I trained the learner on three tasks that share no rules: `L1-S1-M1`, `L2-S2-M2` and `L3-S3-M3`. That made every logic, sensory and motor rule except L4, S4 and M4 known. I then tested tasks where the two hypotheses predict different orderings.\n\n| Test task type | Example | New rules | Mean examples to criterion | n |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| A trained task | `L1-S1-M1` | 0 | ~10 | 4 |\n| Mix of known rules from different trained tasks | `L1-S2-M3` | 0 | ~24 | 17 |\n| Trained task with a new motor rule | `L1-S1-M4` | 1 | ~13 | 16 |\n| Trained task with a new sensory rule | `L1-S4-M1` | 1 | ~15 | 6 |\n| Trained task with a new logic rule | `L4-S1-M1` | 1 | ~32 | 6 |\n| All rules new | `L4-S4-M4` | 3 | ~26 | 5 |\n\n**Why holistic:**\n- **Wrong order for part-based.** Tasks made entirely of known rules cost about as much as the all-new task. They cost clearly more than tasks with one new rule that are otherwise a copy of a trained task. A part-based learner should find the all-known mixes cheapest.\n- **Same count, different cost.** Tasks with exactly one new rule ranged from about 13 to 32 examples, depending on which dimension the new rule was in. Under part-based, only the number of new rules should matter.\n- **What does predict cost:** how close the new task is to one whole trained task.\n\n**Caveats:**\n- **Noise:** single tests ranged from 1 to 49. The main gap (about 24 vs about 13) is roughly 2.4 standard errors, which is why I didn't go higher than 0.85.\n- **Unknown similarity measure:** it isn't simple output overlap. For example, `L1-S2-M3` gives the same response as `L3-S3-M3` on 62.5% of inputs, yet it was still expensive to learn.",
